From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 12:16:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] media: verisilicon: Make the codec run error handling
 consistent

When a codec .run() operation fails, device_run() finishes the job with
hantro_job_finish_no_pm(), but the codec implementations don't agree on
what they have done before returning the error:

- Most of them (G1 H.264/MPEG-2/VP8, VDPU2 H.264/MPEG-2/VP8, G2 HEVC,
  H1 and VEPU2 JPEG) just return. The clocks and the runtime PM
  reference taken by device_run() are then leaked, and the controls of
  the request are never completed, so the request never completes.
- G2 VP9 calls hantro_end_prepare_run(), which completes the request
  controls but also arms the watchdog. Clocks and PM are leaked as
  above, and two seconds later the watchdog fires on whatever job is
  running at that time, reports a bogus timeout, resets the hardware
  under it and finishes it with an error.
- VPU981 AV1 calls hantro_end_prepare_run() and hantro_irq_done(),
  which finishes the job and releases clocks and PM. device_run() then
  finishes the job a second time, returning the next queued buffers
  with an error (or warning when there are none) and calling
  v4l2_m2m_job_finish() for a job that isn't running anymore.

Make the contract explicit and uniform: a .run() that fails must not
have started the hardware, called hantro_end_prepare_run() or
hantro_irq_done(). device_run() then completes the request controls,
disables the clocks, drops the runtime PM reference and finishes the
job with an error, each exactly once. Fix VP9 and AV1 to follow it.

Reference counts on a failing .run(), before and after:

                        clocks  PM usage  request ctrls  job finished
  before, most codecs     +1       +1       pending          once
  before, VP9             +1       +1       completed        once, plus a
                                                             stray watchdog
  before, AV1              0        0       completed        twice
  after, all codecs        0        0       completed        once

v4l2_ctrl_request_complete() is also called on the existing
pm_runtime_resume_and_get() and clk_bulk_enable() failure paths, which
had the same request completion problem. It does nothing for buffers
that aren't part of a request.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
 drivers/media/platform/verisilicon/hantro_drv.c     | 10 +++++++++-
 .../media/platform/verisilicon/hantro_g2_vp9_dec.c  |  4 +---
 drivers/media/platform/verisilicon/hantro_hw.h      |  6 +++++-
 .../verisilicon/rockchip_vpu981_hw_av1_dec.c        | 13 +++----------
 4 files changed, 18 insertions(+), 15 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/verisilicon/hantro_drv.c b/drivers/media/platform/verisilicon/hantro_drv.c
index 4806821e6..2c1ce4010 100644
--- a/drivers/media/platform/verisilicon/hantro_drv.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/verisilicon/hantro_drv.c
@@ -238,13 +238,21 @@ static void device_run(void *priv)
 	v4l2_m2m_buf_copy_metadata(src, dst);
 
 	if (ctx->codec_ops->run(ctx))
-		goto err_cancel_job;
+		goto err_clk_disable;
 
 	return;
 
+err_clk_disable:
+	clk_bulk_disable(core->variant->num_clocks, core->clocks);
 err_pm_put:
 	pm_runtime_put_autosuspend(core->dev);
 err_cancel_job:
+	/*
+	 * The job is cancelled before the controls of its request were
+	 * completed. Complete them now, or the request would never complete.
+	 */
+	v4l2_ctrl_request_complete(src->vb2_buf.req_obj.req,
+				   &ctx->ctrl_handler);
 	hantro_job_finish_no_pm(ctx->dev, ctx, VB2_BUF_STATE_ERROR);
 }
 
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/verisilicon/hantro_g2_vp9_dec.c b/drivers/media/platform/verisilicon/hantro_g2_vp9_dec.c
index 56c79e339..a023e7425 100644
--- a/drivers/media/platform/verisilicon/hantro_g2_vp9_dec.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/verisilicon/hantro_g2_vp9_dec.c
@@ -894,10 +894,8 @@ int hantro_g2_vp9_dec_run(struct hantro_ctx *ctx)
 	int ret;
 
 	ret = start_prepare_run(ctx, &decode_params);
-	if (ret) {
-		hantro_end_prepare_run(ctx);
+	if (ret)
 		return ret;
-	}
 
 	src = hantro_get_src_buf(ctx);
 	dst = hantro_get_dst_buf(ctx);
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/verisilicon/hantro_hw.h b/drivers/media/platform/verisilicon/hantro_hw.h
index 9470f75fb..920721726 100644
--- a/drivers/media/platform/verisilicon/hantro_hw.h
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/verisilicon/hantro_hw.h
@@ -375,7 +375,11 @@ struct hantro_postproc_ops {
  * @run:	Start single {en,de)coding job. Called from atomic context
  *		to indicate that a pair of buffers is ready and the hardware
  *		should be programmed and started. Returns zero if OK, a
- *		negative value in error cases.
+ *		negative value in error cases. On error, the hardware must
+ *		not have been started, and neither hantro_end_prepare_run()
+ *		nor hantro_irq_done() must have been called: the core then
+ *		completes the request controls, releases the clocks and the
+ *		runtime PM reference, and finishes the job with an error.
  * @done:	Read back processing results and additional data from hardware.
  * @reset:	Reset the hardware in case of a timeout.
  */
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/verisilicon/rockchip_vpu981_hw_av1_dec.c b/drivers/media/platform/verisilicon/rockchip_vpu981_hw_av1_dec.c
index 00aa566a4..070809eb8 100644
--- a/drivers/media/platform/verisilicon/rockchip_vpu981_hw_av1_dec.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/verisilicon/rockchip_vpu981_hw_av1_dec.c
@@ -2175,13 +2175,11 @@ int rockchip_vpu981_av1_dec_run(struct hantro_ctx *ctx)
 
 	ret = rockchip_vpu981_av1_dec_prepare_run(ctx);
 	if (ret)
-		goto prepare_error;
+		return ret;
 
 	vb2_src = hantro_get_src_buf(ctx);
-	if (!vb2_src) {
-		ret = -EINVAL;
-		goto prepare_error;
-	}
+	if (!vb2_src)
+		return -EINVAL;
 
 	rockchip_vpu981_av1_dec_clean_refs(ctx);
 	rockchip_vpu981_av1_dec_frame_ref(ctx, vb2_src->vb2_buf.timestamp);
@@ -2229,11 +2227,6 @@ int rockchip_vpu981_av1_dec_run(struct hantro_ctx *ctx)
 	hantro_reg_write(vpu, &av1_dec_e, 1);
 
 	return 0;
-
-prepare_error:
-	hantro_end_prepare_run(ctx);
-	hantro_irq_done(vpu, VB2_BUF_STATE_ERROR);
-	return ret;
 }
 
 static void rockchip_vpu981_postproc_enable(struct hantro_ctx *ctx)
